blue bloods on cbs has a who's who of  talent in the cast not the least of  which is tv legend tom selleck as a  family patriarch frank reagan to one  generation of viewers select may be best  known for his role on magnum pi but his  presence on blue bloods means he's a  rare actor with multiple iconic  television roles to his name many actors  are lucky enough to get just one but  then again selik isn't like most actors  and you can tell that's the case by the  way his co-stars talk about him selik  may have years of acting experience  under his belt far more than any other  performer on blue bloods but donny  wahlberg who plays danny reagan on the  police procedural more than holds his  own against him the two have had ample  scenes together over the years so  suffice to say the two actors have had  numerous opportunities to get to know  each other as expected wahlberg has  nothing but kind things to say about his  coaster considering tom selleck has  decades of experience in the industry it  should come as no surprise to hear that  he's a delight to work with in an  interview with outsider donnie wahlberg  lets it be known that he adores working  alongside the veteran actor he said tom  celek is a leader himself he is a very  experienced very smart guy very  dedicated much like myself there are  times when we both see the hidden game  and we both want the same thing but we  have very different ideas on how to do  it it's a great sensation for an actor  to go up against someone who challenges  them it prevents one from getting  complacent and helps them strive to  deliver another stellar performance  warburg goes on to describe how it's  almost like a dance between the two  saying knowing when to step up knowing  that this is the time tom could really  use my input or knowing when to step  back and knowing this is the time i  gotta trust tom it is very truthful  offscreen as it is on screen for danny  and frank part of the reason why selick  and wolberg get along so well is because  wolberg sees a lot of his own father in  selik's character in a 2013 interview  with the huffington post wahlberg spoke  about what drew him to blue bloods in  the first place as one of nine children  wolberg could relate to his character  dany as one of a group of siblings who  often squabble but always come together  in the end wahlberg said blue bloods is  a throwback show because they don't make  shows that focus on families like this  anymore everything's focused on the  unusual family when i read the pilot the  family dinner scene attracted me most i  felt the heat of my dad staring down at  me and my sister while we're arguing and  that same heat's there with tom selleck  and bridget moynihan and it's not just  the arguments that reminds warburg of  his own family it's the lighter moments  too he said just like we teased tom on  screen that's directly from my childhood  with my dad he was a very tough guy and  we loved trying to break through that  hard exterior and make him laugh  wahlberg and selik's relationship has  deepened in the 11 years since  bluebloods first went on the air in a  recent interview with entertainment  tonight which he gave shortly after the  death of his mother omar wahlberg talked  about his bond with selik i call him dad  now he calls me sun i mean that's how we  talk to each other  with a show like blue bloods it only  works if the actors playing a family can  believably come off that way luckily for  wahlberg and selik it doesn't sound like  they have to fake the father-son dynamic  too much and that's one of the many  reasons why blue bloods has connected so  well with fans  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite movies and tv shows are  coming soon subscribe to our youtube  channel and hit the bell so you don't  miss a single one  [Music]
 

